{"kind":"report","text":"READY-FOR-REVIEW at exact clean head 0fa7474b3cd958c95b0d7349931571fad7a954c1, answering your three findings. Finding 1: lanes now recycle on the Railway.tsx pattern and the rail is capped at ten, Hugh's decision. Re-measured against this workroom's live projection through Vite SSR, the same way you measured: the worst thread, ticket 19, went from 466 lanes and 10276 pixels to ten lanes and 244 pixels, and threads wider than the pane went from 118 of 879 to none. Recycling alone would have left it at 117 lanes, which confirms your judgement that recycling was necessary but not sufficient. My totals are higher than your 714 and 367 because the log has grown since you measured; the shape agrees. The honest cost: 937 of ticket 19's 1174 rows fold, because that thread genuinely has about a hundred concurrent long-lived branches, so any ten-lane rail must fold most of it. 33 of 879 threads fold anything at all. Overflow is shown four ways rather than silently: grey squares instead of coloured circles, dashed parent edges, a banded shared lane, and a per-row folded marker, plus a notice naming the count. Position is lost, provenance is not — every folded row still names its own parent in its text. Finding 2: fourteen mutations were applied, each seen red, then restored; baseline and final are both green. The rendered test also pins the svg width at 244 directly, so an unbounded rail fails on measured width and not only on lane count. Finding 3: the label now tests membership of the laid-out node set, so an off-thread base reads outside this thread, including the root's own basis. Three things I want you to weigh rather than discover. First, closing two of the conditions required a DOM: renderToStaticMarkup runs no effects and emits no handlers, so the click handler and the retarget effect cannot be turned red without one. jsdom 30.0.1 is added as an exact devDependency, test-only, confined to ui/test/dom.test.mjs and absent from dependencies — but it is a dependency decision nobody authorised. Second, sticky folding is my design call, not a requirement: once a branch folds its whole subtree folds. I chose legibility over rail utilisation. Third, ten lanes take 244 of the 384 pixel pane, leaving about 136 pixels for event text on a maximally wide rail; that follows from the ten-lane decision and is worth seeing in the running app. Merging main also required one fix the merge did not flag: main turned kindLabel and kindTint from records into functions taking a vocabulary, so ThreadRailway takes an optional vocabulary prop and ThreadPane passes it, which means declared kinds now render their own names in the rail. Gates: npm test 49 of 49, npm run build clean, the committed embed byte-identical after rebuild, go build and go test across internal and cmd all ok.","body":{"head":"0fa7474b3cd958c95b0d7349931571fad7a954c1"}}